Date: Fri, 30 Apr 1993 03:56:37 -0400 (EDT)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: PHIL: Metaphysics vs. Epistemology, Noumena vs. Phenomena

In the beginning were the ancient Greeks.

They founded Western philosophy.  They did it by asserting the ability of the
human mind to fathom the nature of reality.  It's hard for us, today, with our
telescopes and microscopes and computers and cheap food and central heating, to
appreciate what a leap that was --- for a bunch of half-barbarian peasants
barely out of the Bronze Age to assert that the nature of their huge, hostile
world could be entirely comprehended by a human mind.  There were still lions in
Greece in those days.  And hard weather, and plague, and periodic famine. Life
was, in the main, nasty, brutish and short.

Against the terrors of archaic night, the Greeks erected the worship of mind.
Most of what is worthy in Western civilization descends from that primal,
hubristic assertion --- that man could be the measure, and the measurer, of
all things.

Unfortunately, having made the great imaginative leap that would motivate
philosophy and science for two thousand years, the Greeks overdid it.  They
invented philosophy, then saddled it with philosophical idealism.

In its simplest form, philosophical idealism is akin to the mystic's vision of
the world as one vast thought in the mind of God.  It asserts, that beyond the
contingent and imperfect material world we observe, there a perfect reality
which the philosopher may comprehend by unaided thought.

This is the idealism of Plato.  To a Platonist, the wourld of phenomena is
a distraction.  Only the noumena, the world of ideals or essences, is truly
real and worthy of attention.

This is a very difficult point of view to sustain when a wolf is gnawing at
your leg.  Nevertheless, it has displayed an amazing persistence among German
university professors and other humans sheltered from the more insistent
annoyances of reality, because it feeds the egos of thinkers and humans
generally.  If the reality that matters is only accessible through thought,
thinkers are the true seers and magicians of the world, worthy of awe and
respect and free food.

It is no accident that the pre-Socratics so often sounded as cryptic and
oracular as Heracliteus, nor that the life and death of Socrates himself acted
out the same complex of myths as the martyred ascetic saviors of many
religions.  Philosophical idealism is the mysticism of Mind, cloaked in the
language of reason.

As such, it is not actually very useful for dealing with the world.  Idealism
retains all the drawbacks of mysticism, but retains very little of the
psychological sophistication or insight found in the best forms of mysticism.
It's all very well to worship Mind, but that's little help in seeing the wolf
off.

Thus we come to Aristotle.  He concedes the reality of the world as a given,
but maintains that there is a perfect system of categories and deductions by
which human beings can arrive at the natures of things through unaided
thought.

The Aristotelian worship of logic and reason has most of the same memetic
hooks as Platonism.  It, too, anoints the logician and the reasoner.  Indeed,
because it presents logic as a sort of perfect lever for prying truths
out of the world, it makes a promise Platonism never could --- sign up,
and our logic will catch the *observable* world in a noumenal hammerlock for
you.

There, essentially, is where things stood for over two thousand years.

Aristotelianism left philosophy stuck with a peculiar dualism.  On the one
hand, it is supposed to study something called "metaphysics", which tells us
what the "nature" of reality is.  On the other hand, it is supposed to study
something called "epistemology", which is how we know things about the reality
we observe.

Similarly: Aristotelian tradition identifies rationality with *deductive*
reason. It gives short shrift to observation and induction --- empirical
knowledge.  Aristotle himself "deduced" the wrong count for the teeth in a
human mouth, and apparently never opened one to check.

The separation between "metaphysics" vs. "epistemology", and the parallel
distinction between "rational" and "empirical" knowledge, carries forward the
Platonic dichotomy between noumena and phenomena.

Further: in the Aristotelian tradition, "metaphysics", the search for a perfect
a priori set of categories, is given priority over epistemology, the study of
how we form theories about the observable world.  "Rational" knowledge and the
study of formal systems like logic and geometry is considered prior to
"empirical" knowledge.

Indeed, the Aristotelian prejudice is embedded in our language.  We praise
anyone thinking clearly by saying that they are being "rational".  We do *not*
say they are being "empirical", even when they're clearly working from
observation and induction rather than deductive logic!

Thus, the mysticism of Mind --- the Platonic value judgement that noumenal
truths derived by pure thought and introspection are superior to phenomenal
truths --- is still with us.  It's gone from explicit doctrine in Plato
through implication and method in Aristotle into a pervasive, nearly
invisible prejudice in philosophical tradition and, indeed, everyday language!

It's a symptom of Aristotelianism's hold on our culture that no intellectual
mounted any serious challenge to the Platonic value judgement until the 1700s.
It was left to poets, practical men, and (ironically) mystics to assert the
primacy of experience, until David Hume and John Locke mounted the first
serious empiricist challenges to rationalism.

It's no coincidence that the backdrop to these challenges was the hurly-burly
of the Industrial Revolution.  The rise of technology, engineering and
experimental science was manifestly a triumph of empirical knowledge, the study
of how *observable phenomena* worked.

Indeed, the infant scientific method made empiricism into a system.  *First*
observe, *then* deduce --- then *observe again*.  Theory was verified or
falsified by experiment.  Platonism was turned on its head.  The study of
phenomena, underdog for two millenia, had won; logic and the noumena became
just tools in the engineer's kit.

Philosophy didn't cope with this very gracefully.  Indeed, outside of England
and America it barely coped at all.  It wasn't until 1850 that Charles Peirce
finally published the insight that integrated the lesson of experimental
science into philosophy and settled Platonism's hash for good, founding modern
analytic philosophy in the process.

Peirce's breakthrough was what's now called "operationalism": the equation of
phenomenal "truth" with predictive value, divorcing it from the zero-content
"truth" of mathematics, formal logic and the noumena.

In Peirce's reformulation, the power of scientific method is not a surprise or
an accident, but a definition.  Logic and mathematics and the whole apparatus
of noumenal thinking become contingent; human tools for experimental design,
games that follow the phenomenal world rather than revelations that anticipate
it.

(It is worth noting that he rise of the axiomatic method in mathematics during
the late 19th century brought a lot of mathematicians to Peircean conclusions
about the contingency of logic, albeit by a different route.)

The importance of this astonishing synthesis cannot be overstated.  It is no
exaggeration to call it the single most important event in the history of
Western philosophy.

Unfortunately, the implications of Peirce's work still haven't reached
many corners of our intellectual culture.  There are a number of reasons
for this, primarily historical ones.  The one most important for this 
essay is the split between Anglo-American and Continental philosophy.

While Hume and Locke and their intellectual successors in England and America
were following up the implications of scientific method, philosophy in Europe
remained wedded to the humanities, especially the vernacular belles lettres
that were replacing study of the classics.  There was little place in this
program for science and none for Peirce's radical-empiricist critique.

By the early twentieth century, then, the leading Anglo-American philosophers
generally spoke the language of mathematicians and scientists.  By contrast,
the leading European philosophers were primarily creatures of literature and
politics.  This pattern still holds today.

(Historians of philosophy will know that I'm oversimplifying just a bit
here.  Yes, Wittgenstein and the Vienna Circle were exceptions.  Sort of.
Throw them in the camp I'm calling "Anglo-American" and keep going.)

One of the implications of this split is that the term and concept of
"metaphysics" now has very different statuses in Anglo-American and
Continental philosophy.

For a post-Peircian operationalist, the word "metaphysics" is basically a silly
noise.  The whole idea of a perfect a priori system of categories has gone
out the window along with phlogiston and angelic hierarchies.  We don't
need it nor want it.  Even mathematics is a formalist game now.

In the Continental tradition, on the other hand, metaphysics is still
respectable.  The Aristotelian progression from metaphysics to epistemology,
and the underlying Platonic/rationalist bias for noumena and against
phenomenal/empiricist thinking, is still accepted as the natural and correct
way to do philosophy.

The result is that Continental philosophers generally do wretchedly bad
epistemology.  Anglo-American philosophers do epistemology very well, but
seldom do anything else.

				*****

Now.  I was asked to write this essay to explain some criticisms of Ayn Rand 
that I'd made; specifically, that Objectivist epistemology fails because
it's founded on a belief that there is a `metaphysics' separable from
epistemology.

Now I can explain that statement.  Ayn Rand was a continental philosopher.
She didn't know from epistemology; she spoke literature and politics.

It's significant that she grew up in Russia, and formed her system in emulation
of Stirner and reaction against Kant and Hegel.  Both her influences and her
enemies were German idealists.  Communism, the political monster that destroyed
her childhood, was the creation of another one, Marx.

The living Anglo-American tradition of empiricism was unknown to her.  So she
got out from under idealism by becoming the last great Aristotelian.

Unfortunately this meant that her thinking was still conditioned by the
Aristotelian belief in the priority of formal logic and noumenal thinking.
If you're hip to the technicalia involved, it's a sad and funny experience
to read _Introduction_To_Objectivist_Epistemology_ and see exactly where
she goes off the rails.

She starts off with good phenomenological analysis --- primacy of experience,
evidence of the senses, all the right stuff.  Unfortunately, what she's really
hot to do is bootstrap herself off the phenomenal level and hang out in the
noumenal world of Aristotelian logic and perfect categories.

How does she do it?  With the "Law of Identity".

In Objectivism's own terms, A = A is the ultimate "stolen premise" --- a
tautology from formal logic which gets turned into the *metaphysical* assertion
that it's always valid to reason about the world in Aristotelian predicates.

This early *metaphysical* assertion is the root of everything that's rigid,
cramped, dogmatic, exclusive, fanatically two-valued and fundamentally
doomed about Objectivism.

It's also the reason that anyone who understands the Peircean breakthrough
can't manage to take Objectivist epistemology seriously for ten seconds.  We've
known better than to confuse phemonenal/predictive truth with noumenal/formal
"truth" for a century and a half.  Rand's "Law of Identity" is a category
error of the most fatal and obvious kind.

And that's a damned shame.  Because there's so much else about Rand's
system that's worthy, and powerful, and valuable.  Her ethical philosophy,
especially the analysis-to-destruction of altruism and collectivism.  Her
exhortation to live according to your highest values.  Her celebration
of creativity and reason.  Her steadfast assertion of individual rights
and responsibility.

All of this *could* have been grounded in a solid post-Peircean operationalism.

I've tried to imagine how Rand's thought might have developed if she'd avoided
that Aristotelian trap.  For example, suppose she'd learned epistemology from
her contemporary Alfred Korzybski, the founder of General Semantics.  Ideas
really mattered in her life; perhaps, under empiricist influence, she wouldn't
have rigidified into an autocratic, cast-iron bitch.

Possibly she'd have then avoided degenerating into a cult leader, scarfing down
amphetamines, speaking in hieratic code to a coterie of disciples, and unable
to communicate to the rest of the world well enough to lead a true movement for
freedom.  Maybe she wouldn't have died of lung cancer from her "rational"
smoking habit.  Maybe she'd still be alive.

Truly, it sometimes makes me heartsick to think what "A=A" may have lost us.
