GS's politics can probably be boiled down to "technocracy".  This
reflects the political prejudices of Korzybski more than any necessary
connection to the core of GS.  Many GS followers (eg Heinlein, 
Hayakawa) tended to be more libertarian.  In turn Korzybski is
basically following in the footsteps of Saint-Simon, Comte, Wells,
Russel, etc. which dominated scientist's political opinions at that
time.

> Essentials of Extropian Philosophy [More]
> -----------------------------------------
> Metaphysics  - scientific reality with emphasis on (local) reversals
>                of entropy....
> Politics     - anarcho-capitalism, PPL, spontaneous order...

These three philosophies by no means cover the range of viewpoints on
this list, and specifically they do not cover mine, which is at the same 
time group-oriented (not coercively collectivist, and not individualist), 
anarcho-capitalist, anti-entropic, and empirical.  There is no necessary 
reason to focus on *individual* negentropy; it makes more sense to me to
focus on the negentropy of the meme, since it is the relative value
of memes we are trying to determine here.  Furthermore, for all its 
promotion of rationality and negentropy, Extropianism has so far shied
away from objectifying the reversal of entropy, even for the individual, 
preferring subjective ideas like "self-interest" which have no necessary 
connection to negentropy.  This may be because so many subjective beliefs 
have been built into the "Extropian Principles" and related documents 
which might be falsified in the face of objective evidence that they do 
not in fact maximize negentropy.  

Extropianism seems to have borrowed individualism straight from Rand 
and Nietchze; individualism has no necessary connection to negentropy 
(just as technocracy has no necessary connection to the rest of GS).  
I claim that individualism does *not* minimize entropy, either individual 
or meme-wide, and  I will be happy to argue this claim empirically if any 
individualists care to engage in an empirical debate on the matter (ie 
with facts, measures of negentropy, not opinions or anecdotes).  I
further claim that rationality does not necessarily 
maximize negentropy, although rationality is needed to step back and
analyze the entropic consequences of a meme.

>From negentropy follows at least one other aspect where I am in
accord with Extropianism: anarcho-capitalism.  Unfortunately, the
link between negentropy and anarcho-capitalism has never been
explicitly demonstrated.  I think we can recast the arguments in 
_Machinery of Freedom_ to show that anarcho-capitalism optimizes 
negentropy, rather than focusing on subjective "utility".  This
will complete the defeat of Saint-Simon/Comte socialism, which claimed
the same goal as Extropianism -- maximizing negentropy, but brought in
a very different set of memetic baggage about "class", ad nauseum which 
was never empirically verified.  (cf. Hayek, _The Counter-Revolution of
Science_).

Although David Friedman often takes an individualist slant, his examples 
depend primarily on large collective groups like corporations behaving in 
their "self-interest", rather than individuals.  In fact Friedman explicitly 
states that voluntary collectives like communes are perfectly compatible 
with anarcho-capitalism.  I'd add that small-scale non-market relationships, 
eg the family, informal reciprocal altruism, and informal reputations are 
also quite important.  Some individualist Extropians, by bashing rather 
than promoting these anti-entropic collectives, promote the increase rather 
than decrease of entropy.  Again, this is a matter that can be empirically 
verified or falsified; it need not remain a matter of mere faith or 
Principles.  The failed attempts to create "reputation markets" on
this list is one demonstration of how informal reputation systems can
be more efficient and productive than formal ones.  If we do not take
care to empirically verify our beliefs, they may cause the trouble
and reach bad ends as Saint-Simon's did (Marxism, Fabian Socialism, ad 
nauseum).

I'd like to see some numbers comparing the memetic negentropy 
of a meme, meaning the total entropic impact of a meme on its 
hosts.  I've posted my suggestions for negentropic benchmarks 
before (eg lifespan, fertility, education level, economic wealth, 
self-replicating robot model, etc.); I'd like to see
other folks' ideas on this, and would be greatly interested in 
analysis based on physics and/or information theory.


