  I would like to make a small point about the above. I agree with most
of what Eric is saying (although I disagree about the usefullness
of GS).

  Mathematics is not always pure deduction, in fact, a lot of mathematics
originated from empirical observations about numeric systems. For instance,
a great amount of number theory was developed by hobbyists just ``playing''
around with numbers and looking for useful patterns. One could
try to determine what conditions must exist for a number to be divisible
by 3. The ``experiment'' consists of performing permutations on the digits
of numbers and the number itself. (adding them, subtracting, multiplying,etc).
After lots of trials, one proposes a hypothesis: if the sum of digits
is divisible by 3, then the number itself is divisible by 3. Many
people discover this law on their own. The formalism and deduction
comes in later when you try to prove this hypothesis. The main
difference between the physical sciences and mathematics is that mathematics
can define its own universe so there's no need to discover how it
[the universe] works, all you need to do is organize it better with theorems.
(which you can the prove absolutely, unlike physical sciences where
proof only lasts until something is disproved. However, some
conjectures in mathematics, like Fermat's Last Theorem, may only
be provable by empiricism.)

  What I find absolutely amazing about mathematics is its uncanny
ability to agree with non-intuitive reality even when developed in
a purely abstract way. Knot theory, Category theory, Differential
Topology -- abstract theories which really weren't developed to explain
reality, but theoretical physicists are applying them today in new
models. Doesn't anyone find it absolutely amazing that Einstein's
General Relativity, which was developed using pretty reasonable
postulates, taken to extreme scales still agrees with experiment?
Or Atomic theory, developed long before we had powerful particle accelerators
or STMs, agrees closely with what we have found emprically. QED, where
a few simple assumptions lead to predictions that agree with experimental
value to incredible precision.

(digression: This may sound strange, but some mathematicians actually dislike
the idea of a theory having practical applications. To them, pure math
is beautiful, not to be sullied by mundane real world applications.)

On a side note:

  There is a shakeup happening in the world of quantum mechanics
right now. Aharonov, is now claiming that the wavefunction psi,
which was just a conveinent mathematical tool, has a direct physical
meaning in a _single particle context_ which can be measured 
_without disturbing the particle it is related to_. Furthermore, 
Bennett (Quantum Cryptography, Logical Depth) has just published a paper on 
Quantum Teleportation. His discovery is that information about an unknown 
quantum state can be separated into a classical component and a quantum
component. The classical component can only be sent sublight, but the quantum
component can be transmitted (via EPR) instantaneously. The two signals can 
be combined at a distant point, yielding a _perfect_ reconstruction of
the original unknown state.
(I haven't seen the paper, this is anecdotal so far from what I 
read from the net)
  This seems to be an incredible result. A normal quantum state
contains an infinite amount of information (precision), but by
sending only a finite classical value,  I can actually transmit
the whole quantum state. This is like rounding off values in computer
arithmetic but being able to get answers that have arbitrary
precision. 

   All of this was developed using formal mathematics, but I am willing to
bet that it will be confirmed by experiment. And that is what I find
amazing. 

  
> (It is worth noting that he rise of the axiomatic method in mathematics during
> the late 19th century brought a lot of mathematicians to Peircean conclusions
> about the contingency of logic, albeit by a different route.)

Afterwards Kurt Godel shattered this idea of "axiomatizing" all of mathematics
in his ground breaking paper.

> In Objectivism's own terms, A = A is the ultimate "stolen premise" --- a
> tautology from formal logic which gets turned into the *metaphysical* assertion
