  I would say that there is a middle ground between absolute empiricism
and absolute formalism.  One certainly can't go around conducting
experiments for every prediction your mind comes up with because you
couldn't get anything done. (e.g. judging from some fundamental axioms
that I have internalized about the world, I predict that jumping in front
of a car will endanger my life.)  However, one also can't go around
the world expecting everything to obey simplistic principles and categories
your mind has come up with. (e.g. Objectivism syndrome. Using a few
metaphysical propositions, I can prove or disprove the truth of 
everything formally)  I imagine a better philosophical system which
goes like ``use what you know already to reason things about reality,
but continually learn about the world empricially so you have a larger
knowledge base for your predictions -- and therefore a smaller probability
of being wrong.'' Addition: ``When your predictions come under fire, try 
putting them to a test first (i.e. question them), and if they
pass the test, you have a stronger position. If they fail, you have learned
something new.''

